<figure class="wp-block-image alignright is-resized is-style-rounded"><img decoding="async" width="600" height="600" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul.jpg" alt="Aysegul Sanford from the front view." class="wp-image-84391" style="width:200px;height:200px" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul.jpg 600w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul-400x400.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul-150x150.jpg 150w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul-500x500.jpg 500w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul-300x300.jpg 300w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul-96x96.jpg 96w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul-378x378.jpg 378w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul-276x276.jpg 276w" sizes="(max-width: 600px) 100vw, 600px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul.jpg"></figure>



<p>Growing up in T&uuml;rkiye, our holiday tables looked a little different than the ones we have here in the US. Kebabs were always on the menu, but whenever my mom wanted to make something special, she made these ground beef stuffed onions, or <em>so&#287;an dolmas</em>&#305; as we call it.</p>



<p>It has been years since I last made them, but seeing their recent popularity on social media made me pull out my mom&rsquo;s handwritten notes and bring this recipe back to life.</p>



<p>Here are a few things that make this version special:</p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>It has roots in Turkish cooking.</strong> There are many versions of stuffed onions across the Mediterranean and the Middle East, but the Turkish style uses pomegranate molasses to give the dish a gentle, sweet-and-tangy balance.</li>



<li><strong>The ingredients are simple.</strong> Pomegranate molasses might sound unfamiliar, but most grocery stores now carry it. Everything else is a pantry staple, and I included plenty of substitutions so you can make it work with what you have on hand.</li>



<li><strong>It looks impressive, but it is easier than it seems.</strong> Once you soften the onions and get the hang of rolling them, the rest of the process is surprisingly approachable and very forgiving.</li>
</ul>



<p>If you have never made stuffed onions before, I think you will be pleasantly surprised by how doable this dish actually is.</p>

</div>


<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="h-ingredients-you-ll-need">Ingredients You&rsquo;ll Need</h2>



<p>If you&rsquo;ve been following along on social media, you&rsquo;ve probably seen me testing this recipe quite a bit. As I worked through each round, I learned a few things about choosing the right ingredients. </p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="800" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Stuffed-Onions-Ingredients-1200x800.jpg" alt="Ingredients for the recipe from the top view." class="wp-image-85236" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Stuffed-Onions-Ingredients.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Stuffed-Onions-Ingredients-400x267.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Stuffed-Onions-Ingredients-768x512.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Stuffed-Onions-Ingredients-150x100.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Stuffed-Onions-Ingredients.jpg"></figure>



<p>The exact measurements are in the recipe card below, but these are the notes I kept from all that testing:</p>



<p><strong>Onions:</strong> Look for onions that are firm, fresh, and heavy for their size. Yellow onions work beautifully because they naturally become sweeter as they cook, but Vidalia onions and Walla Walla onions will also work if you want an even milder flavor. Red onions are also an option, but stuffed red onions are slightly sweeter than yellow onions.</p>


<div class="block-tip cwp-inner is-style-border has-background has-senary-background-color">

<p><strong>Pro tip: </strong>In the recipe card, you&rsquo;ll see four medium onions. If you&rsquo;re making this for the first time, go ahead and buy one extra just in case. Depending on the size, four to five onions usually give me about 20 to 22 usable layers, which is right around what you&rsquo;ll need for this stuffed onion recipe.</p>

</div>


<p><strong>Ground beef: </strong>Fat equals flavor, so I like using 85/15 ground beef because it stays juicy and tender. If you prefer something richer, use 80/20 ground beef.&nbsp;</p>



<p>You can also use lamb if you like, or half lamb and half beef, which is pretty traditional. If you are looking for a lighter alternative, though not traditional, ground chicken would also work, but be aware that it would have a slightly different flavor profile.</p>



<p><strong>White rice: </strong>I&rsquo;m using long-grain white rice here, the everyday enriched kind that you can find in most grocery stores.&nbsp;</p>



<p>My mom always rinsed the rice under cold water until it ran clear, so I do that too, but I know some people prefer not to wash their rice, and that&rsquo;s perfectly fine.</p>



<p>Some people soak their rice before using it, which helps it cook a little faster, but since we are using a rather small amount, I do not think that it is necessary.&nbsp;</p>



<p>Alternatively, medium <a href="https://foolproofliving.com/what-is-bulgur-wheat/">bulgur wheat</a> can be a good substitute for rice. It would have a distinct earthy flavor profile, but it would still be delicious.</p>



<p><strong>Pomegranate molasses:</strong> Can you make it without it? You surely can. But, should you? Absolutely not, because pomegranate molasses takes these stuffed onions to another level, giving them the signature slightly sweet-and-sour taste. So, do not skip it.</p>


<div class="block-tip cwp-inner is-style-border has-background has-senary-background-color">

<p><strong>Pro Tip:</strong> When shopping for it, look for a brand that uses 100% pomegranate juice and no added sugars. If you can&rsquo;t find it in your local grocery stores&rsquo; Middle Eastern section, look for it online. My favorite one is this <a href="https://amzn.to/48JDm7k" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener nofollow">Pomegranate Molasses</a>.</p>

</div>


<p><strong>Red pepper paste:</strong> This is known as <em>biber sal&ccedil;as&#305;</em> in Turkish cooking. You can find mild or hot versions, and both work here. If you can&rsquo;t find it, you can <strong>use an equal amount of tomato paste as a substitute</strong>, and it will still be delicious.</p>



<p>Most Middle Eastern supermarkets carry it, but if you can&rsquo;t find it there, you can buy it online. I like <a href="https://amzn.to/44nZDX0" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener nofollow">Oncu Turkish Mild Pepper Paste</a>, which has great depth without being overly spicy.</p>



<p><strong>Spices:</strong> This part of the recipe is the most flexible, and you can get as creative as you want. I use ground cumin, dried mint, black pepper, and red pepper flakes for a little heat.</p>



<p>If you can&rsquo;t get your hands on dried mint, you can omit using it or use a tablespoon of chopped fresh mint instead.</p>
</div>



<div class="wp-block-group is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="h-how-to-make-stuffed-onions">How to Make Stuffed Onions</h2>



<p>If this is your first time making stuffed onions with meat, and it looks like a lot of work, let me assure you, it is not. Once you start stuffing and rolling your first couple of onions, you&rsquo;ll see quickly that the whole process is actually quite simple.</p>



<p>Below, I am sharing every step of the process with the little things I learned from my mom and from making this Mediterranean dish over the years for my own family:</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Stuffed-onions-with-ground-beef.jpg" alt="A collage of images showing the preparation of the onions to make stuffed onions." class="wp-image-85237" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Stuffed-onions-with-ground-beef.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Stuffed-onions-with-ground-beef-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Stuffed-onions-with-ground-beef-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Stuffed-onions-with-ground-beef-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Stuffed-onions-with-ground-beef.jpg"></figure>



<p><strong>Step 1 &ndash; Prep the onions: </strong>If there&rsquo;s one part to pay a little extra attention to, it&rsquo;s this step. We&rsquo;re trying to separate the layers in one piece as much as we can. Even if a few tear, don&rsquo;t worry, they still work just fine once they&rsquo;re stuffed.</p>



<p>Start by cutting off both ends of each onion, peeling the outer skin, and making a single straight cut down to the center without slicing all the way through. Doing this helps the layers loosen easily after boiling. Use a sharp knife here to make it easier on yourself.</p>



<p><strong>Step 2 &ndash; Boil the onions:</strong> Add the onions to a large pot of water, bring it to a boil, and let it simmer for about 15 minutes.&nbsp; You don&rsquo;t want them overly soft, just soft enough that they don&rsquo;t rip when you start separating the layers later.&nbsp;</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Beef-stuffed-onions.jpg" alt="A collage of images showing the onion layers and the beef and rice filling." class="wp-image-85238" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Beef-stuffed-onions.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Beef-stuffed-onions-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Beef-stuffed-onions-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Beef-stuffed-onions-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Beef-stuffed-onions.jpg"></figure>



<p><strong>Step 3 &ndash; Let them cool and separate the layers:</strong> Spread the onions out on a plate and let them cool on the counter.</p>


<div class="block-tip cwp-inner is-style-border has-background has-senary-background-color">

<p><strong>Pro Tip:</strong> It takes a while to cool down, especially the inner layers. If you are short on time, you can place them in a colander and run them under cold tap water to speed up cooling. However, it is imperative that you let them drain properly before you start stuffing and rolling them.</p>

</div>


<p>When cool enough to handle, carefully remove each layer of the onion, being gentle to avoid tearing it. You should have about 20-22 onion layers in the end.&nbsp;</p>



<p>As you peel off each layer, you&rsquo;ll notice a thin membrane. If you have the time, remove it; it helps the onions cook more evenly and prevents any chewiness. But if you&rsquo;re in a pinch, you can skip this step. I&rsquo;ve made this dish plenty of times, leaving the membrane on, and it still turns out great.</p>



<p>As you get closer to the middle, you&rsquo;ll end up with the onion cores. Don&rsquo;t throw them out. We&rsquo;ll use them in the filling, so put them in a large bowl.</p>



<p><strong>Step 4 &ndash; Mix the filling:</strong> Preheat the oven to 400&deg;F (204&deg;C), and while it heats, make the filling. Add the ground beef, rice, red pepper paste, tomato paste, all of the spices and seasoning, pomegranate molasses, and parsley to the bowl with the chopped onion cores.</p>



<p>Using clean hands, give it a good mix so that everything is evenly combined. You&rsquo;re looking for a mixture that feels slightly sticky and moist but holds together when you press it between your fingers.&nbsp;</p>



<p><strong>Step 5 &ndash; Roll each layer into a little stuffed onion (aka dolma):</strong> Place about 1 &frac12; tablespoons of filling inside each layer and roll it tightly.</p>


<div class="block-tip cwp-inner is-style-border has-background has-senary-background-color">

<p><strong>Pro Tip:</strong> As you stuff and roll, you&rsquo;ll see that the very outermost layers of the onion are longer than the inner layers. I find that stuffed onions made with these longer layers are a bit chewy, so I cut them in half. This way, all layers are roughly the same size, and every stuffed onion tastes the same after everything is baked.</p>

</div>


<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Onions-stuffed-with-ground-beef.jpg" alt="Stuffed onion in a skillet with the sauce." class="wp-image-85247" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Onions-stuffed-with-ground-beef.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Onions-stuffed-with-ground-beef-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Onions-stuffed-with-ground-beef-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Onions-stuffed-with-ground-beef-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Onions-stuffed-with-ground-beef.jpg"></figure>



<p><strong>Step 6 &ndash; Arrange them in the pan: </strong>I am using a <a href="https://amzn.to/4iTmKPq" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener nofollow">3 &frac12;-quart cast iron skillet</a>, but a large casserole dish would also work well.&nbsp;</p>



<p>Place the stuffed onions in a spiral, starting from the outer edge, tucking each one right up against the next.</p>



<p>You want them close enough to support each other without squeezing them too tightly. In my 12-inch pan, depending on the size of my onions, I can usually fit 18-22 stuffed onions in one spiral layer.</p>



<p><strong>Step 7 &ndash; Make the sauce: </strong>In a measuring cup, combine olive oil, pomegranate molasses, tomato paste, red pepper paste, garlic, salt, and boiling water. Whisk until everything dissolves.&nbsp;</p>



<p>Pour the sauce right over the onions. You can cover the pan with foil or a large sheet of parchment paper.</p>


<div class="block-tip cwp-inner is-style-border has-background has-senary-background-color">

<p><strong>Pro Tip: </strong>During my recipe testing, I kept thinking the sauce looked like too much. It isn&rsquo;t. You need that liquid to help the onions and filling cook slowly and evenly, and most of it evaporates in the oven, leaving just the right amount behind.</p>

</div>


<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Stuffed-baked-onions.jpg" alt="Stuffed roasted before and after they are baked." class="wp-image-85249" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Stuffed-baked-onions.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Stuffed-baked-onions-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Stuffed-baked-onions-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Stuffed-baked-onions-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Stuffed-baked-onions.jpg"></figure>



<p><strong>Step 8 &ndash; Bake: </strong>Place the onions in the oven, covered, for about 30 minutes to soften all the way through. Then uncover the dish and let them brown and caramelize around the edges for another 20 minutes or so.</p>



<p>You&rsquo;ll see that the remaining liquid will be thicker and reduced almost in half.</p>



<p><strong>Step 9 &ndash; Finish with fresh parsley and serve: </strong>I love adding a handful of parsley at the end to brighten the entire dish. Serve your baked stuffed onions warm, with extra sauce spooned over the top.</p>
</div>



